 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed once [G]. Hash map operations are $O(1)$ on average [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices, proportional to the number of voxels with sign changes [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. [S1, unofficial]
-- **Scaling:** Scales linearly with voxel count. Performance is limited by memory bandwidth for accessing scalar values and gradients [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple implementation, fast, no complex solver required [G].
-- **Limitations:** Vertices are fixed at cell centers, leading to potential geometric inaccuracies. Topology is not guaranteed to be manifold or correct in complex cases. [G] Face orientation depends on strict basis vector ordering [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When speed and simplicity are prioritized over geometric accuracy and topological robustness. [G] Suitable for simple scalar fields or when used as a base for more advanced methods [G].
-- **When not to use:** When high geometric fidelity or guaranteed manifold topology is required. [G]
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels in the bounds, as each voxel is processed once for vertex placement and a constant number of quad checks.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of extracted vertices and quads. The `vertex_map` requires memory proportional to the number of active vertices.
+*   **Performance:** Fast due to the absence of iterative QEF solving. However, the resulting mesh may have topological errors or geometric inaccuracies compared to full Dual Contouring [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, no need for complex solvers, fast execution.
+*   **Limitations:** Potential for topological inaccuracies, fixed vertex placement may not capture fine geometric details as well as QEF-based methods, quad-based output requires post-processing for triangle meshes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When speed is critical and approximate geometry is acceptable, or as a baseline for comparison.
+*   **When not to use:** When high geometric fidelity or strict topological correctness is required.
 
 ## Background
-Simplified Dual Contouring is an unfinished attempt at general-purpose dual-contouring isosurface extraction, as described in an unofficial GitHub repository [S1, unofficial]. It references tutorials and blogs from 2016-2018 [S1, unofficial]. The method is presented as a simpler alternative to full Dual Contouring, omitting the QEF minimization step [S1, unofficial].
+Simplified Dual Contouring is an informal implementation attempt described in an unofficial GitHub repository [S1, unofficial]. It draws inspiration from general dual-contouring tutorials and voxel-based surface extraction techniques [S1, unofficial]. The method is presented as an "unfinished" general-purpose approach, highlighting its experimental nature [S1, unofficial].
 
